# `random:integer_stream`'s loop is just `loop { output.send(...)?; }`, with no
# separate, hand-rolled job-control controller of its own -- which the design's
# command inventory read, from the code shape alone, as "doesn't cooperate at all".
# That reading was wrong: `output` comes from `context.initialize_output(...)`,
# which already registers a controller (see `CommandContext::initialize_output` in
# src/lang/state/contexts.rs), and `TableOutputStream::send` already `select!`s
# against that controller's channel on every single call (src/lang/pipe.rs) --
# Terminate/Pause are handled right there, every iteration, with no extra code
# needed. Corrected after Task 4 found the same `initialize_output`/`poll_control`
# mechanism while fixing `fs:usage` and `types.file.remove`'s *different* gap (those
# two don't call `send` for every entry, so they needed an explicit extra check
# between entries -- this command calls `send` every iteration already).
#
# Confirmed directly across several real scenarios: backgrounded with no consumer,
# backgrounded piped into `count` (an active, indefinite drain) then `pause`d, and
# foregrounded then Ctrl-Z'd. In every case, `host:procs`' own `cpu` column (total
# CPU time used) was measured flat across a 1-second window once paused/terminated,
# and `crush:threads` showed no thread left tracked under the job after `terminate`.
# There was no failing test to drive a Rust change here (the TDD Iron Law: no
# production code without a failing test first) -- this is a regression guard, not
# a bug fix, same treatment as fs:watch/remote:pexec get in this plan.
source tests/pty_test_helpers.crush
$t := $(pty_spawn)

$t:run "random:integer_stream | count &"
sleep $(duration:of milliseconds=300)
$cpu_running := $(host:procs | where {($pid == ($t:pid()))} | select cpu)[0]:cpu

$t:run "pause 0"
$cpu_a := $(host:procs | where {($pid == ($t:pid()))} | select cpu)[0]:cpu
sleep $(duration:of milliseconds=500)
$cpu_b := $(host:procs | where {($pid == ($t:pid()))} | select cpu)[0]:cpu
assert ($cpu_b <= ($cpu_a + $(duration:of milliseconds=50))) $("expected no measurable CPU growth while paused, got {} -> {} (was {} while running)":format $cpu_a $cpu_b $cpu_running)

$t:run "resume 0"
$t:run "terminate 0"
pty_assert_job_status $t 0 "Terminated"
pty_assert_no_thread $t 0

$t:close

# Uninterrupted case must keep working identically to before this plan.
$rows := $(random:integer_stream 100 | head 5 | count)
assert ($rows == 5) "random:integer_stream must still work normally when never interrupted"

echo "ok"
